.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH kage 1  "kage 0.1.0" 
.SH NAME
kage \- A minimal, extensible coding agent in your terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBkage\fR [\fB\-p\fR|\fB\-\-print\fR] [\fB\-m\fR|\fB\-\-model\fR] [\fB\-\-system\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-session\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fIsubcommands\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no command and no \fB\-p\fR, kage opens its interactive TUI in the current directory. Type a prompt and press enter to send it. On an empty prompt, \fB?\fR shows the keys and \fB/\fR opens the command palette. \fBctrl+q\fR quits.
.PP
With \fB\-p\fR, kage runs one prompt through the agent loop, streams the reply to stdout and exits.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-print\fR \fI<PRINT>\fR
Run a single prompt through the agent loop and stream the response to stdout
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-model\fR \fI<MODEL>\fR
Provider\-qualified model id (`provider:model`). Defaults to `[provider] default_model` when its provider has credentials, then the last model you used, then the first available provider\*(Aqs preferred model
.TP
\fB\-\-system\fR \fI<SYSTEM>\fR [default: You are kage, a helpful coding agent.]
System prompt to prepend
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-session\fR
Disable session recording. By default every run writes a JSONL session file under `$XDG_DATA_HOME/kage/sessions/<session\-id>.jsonl`
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit one JSON object per event on stdout instead of plain text. Only meaningful with `\-p/\-\-print`. Each line carries the event\*(Aqs `type` (such as `text_delta`, `tool_call_start`, `message_appended`, or `run_ended`) plus the `session` it belongs to and a per\-session `seq` number, so external tools can parse the agent\*(Aqs output without screen\-scraping
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBkage list\fR
List recorded sessions in `$XDG_DATA_HOME/kage/sessions/`
.TP
\fBkage resume\fR
Resume a recorded session, appending new entries to the same file
.TP
\fBkage fork\fR
Fork a recorded session at a specific entry into a new session file
.TP
\fBkage search\fR
Regex\-search recorded sessions
.TP
\fBkage auth\fR
Manage saved provider API credentials
.TP
\fBkage auth login\fR
Save an API key for a provider, prompting for it without echo
.TP
\fBkage auth logout\fR
Remove a saved API key
.TP
\fBkage auth list\fR
Show which known and custom providers have credentials available
.TP
\fBkage init\fR
First\-run setup wizard. Creates `~/.config/kage/config.toml`, scaffolds the data directories, and offers to save a provider API key. Idempotent: rerunning without `\-\-force` keeps any existing config in place
.TP
\fBkage doctor\fR
Diagnose the kage install: parses config, lists available providers, validates plugins, reports the active sandbox. Exit code is `0` when no check fails, `1` otherwise
.TP
\fBkage completions\fR
Print a shell completion script for `kage` to stdout
.TP
\fBkage rpc\fR
Agent Client Protocol server: speak JSON\-RPC over stdio so an editor (Zed, Neovim, ...) can drive kage. Messages are newline\-delimited JSON. Each request is answered, and loop progress is streamed back as ACP `session/update` notifications
.TP
\fBkage trust\fR
Trust the current directory\*(Aqs `.kage/config.toml`. Until a project is trusted, its `mcp`, `permissions` and `plugins.capabilities` settings are ignored. Trust covers the values as they are now, so editing any of them asks again
.TP
\fBkage models\fR
Manage the model catalog. kage ships a snapshot of models.dev; `kage models refresh` fetches a newer one into the model cache, which later runs use in place of the snapshot\*(Aqs model entries
.TP
\fBkage models refresh\fR
Download the models.dev catalog into `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/kage/models.json`. It can add models and update their metadata, never provider endpoints or credentials. Only runs when asked; kage never refreshes on its own
.TP
\fBkage mcp\fR
Model Context Protocol: expose kage\*(Aqs built\-in tools to another agent over stdio (newline\-delimited JSON\-RPC) with `kage mcp serve`, or log in to remote MCP servers that need OAuth
.TP
\fBkage mcp serve\fR
Serve kage\*(Aqs built\-in tools as an MCP server over stdio. Calls are checked against `[permissions]`, and a tool whose verdict is `ask` is refused because there is no one to ask
.TP
\fBkage mcp login\fR
Log in to a remote MCP server with OAuth. Prints the authorization URL, opens it in a browser when one is available, and waits for the browser to return, or for the redirected URL to be pasted on a machine without one. The token is stored in `$XDG_DATA_HOME/kage/mcp\-auth.json` (mode 0600)
.TP
\fBkage mcp logout\fR
Forget the stored OAuth token of a remote MCP server
.PP
Run \fBkage \fR\fIcommand\fR\fB \-\-help\fR for the options of a command.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI~/.config/kage/config.toml\fR
User config, written by kage init.
.TP
\fI~/.config/kage/init.lua\fR
Lua config the TUI runs after config.toml.
.TP
\fI<workdir>/.kage/config.toml\fR
Project config, merged over the user config. Its mcp, permissions and plugins.capabilities tables apply only after kage trust.
.TP
\fI~/.config/kage/{plugins,themes,agents,skills,templates}/\fR
Lua plugins, user themes, agent definitions, skills and prompt templates.
.TP
\fI~/.local/share/kage/sessions/\fR
Recorded sessions, one JSONL file each.
.TP
\fI~/.local/share/kage/auth.json\fR
Saved provider credentials (mode 0600).
.TP
\fI~/.local/state/kage/\fR
Session state, input history and trusted projects (trust.json).
.TP
\fI~/.cache/kage/models.json\fR
Model catalog written by kage models refresh.
.PP
XDG_CONFIG_HOME, XDG_DATA_HOME, XDG_STATE_HOME and XDG_CACHE_HOME replace the ~/.config, ~/.local/share, ~/.local/state and ~/.cache roots.
.SH VERSION
v0.1.0
